#[path = "solution.rs"]
mod solution;
use solution::total_column;

#[test]
fn zero_based_maximum_column_index_is_representable() {
    let mut number = usize::MAX as u128 + 1;
    let mut reversed = Vec::new();
    while number > 0 {
        number -= 1;
        reversed.push(b'A' + (number % 26) as u8);
        number /= 26;
    }
    reversed.reverse();
    let label = String::from_utf8(reversed).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(total_column(&[], &label), Ok(Vec::new()));
}

prompt.text:
Implement a standard-library-only Rust library for totaling an in-memory grid column across named tables.

Expose this exact API from `solution.rs`:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub enum Cell {
    Number(f64),
    Text(String),
    Empty,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub struct Table {
    pub name: String,
    pub rows: Vec<Vec<Cell>>,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub struct ColumnTotal {
    pub table_name: String,
    pub present: bool,
    pub sum: f64,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ColumnError {
    InvalidLabel,
    LabelOverflow,
}

pub fn total_column(tables: &[Table], label: &str) -> Result<Vec<ColumnTotal>, ColumnError>;
```

A column label is a nonempty sequence of uppercase ASCII letters using spreadsheet-style numbering: `A` is index 0, `Z` is 25, `AA` is 26. Empty labels, lowercase letters, digits, whitespace, and punctuation produce `InvalidLabel`. If conversion cannot fit in `usize`, return `LabelOverflow`.

For each table, preserve input ordering and return one result. `present` is true if at least one row has a cell at the selected index, regardless of that cell's variant. Sum only `Number` cells; ignore text and empty cells. Missing cells in short rows are ignored. If the column is absent, set `present` to false and `sum` to `0.0`. Empty input returns an empty vector. Do not mutate the tables.

